\section{Results}
\label{sec:results}

\subsection{Weakest-Link Dominance at \texorpdfstring{$K{=}3$}{K=3}}
\label{sec:dominance}

Table~\ref{tab:dominance} reports the log-log OLS regression (Equation~\ref{eq:regression}) for all 12 conditions across the 455 panels ($K{=}3$, 15 judges from Table~\ref{tab:models}).

\begin{table}[t]
\centering
\small
\begin{tabular}{llccc}
\toprule
\textbf{Bench.} & \textbf{Attack} & $\boldsymbol{\eta_{\max}}$ & $\boldsymbol{K_{\mathrm{eff}}}$ & \textbf{Dom.} \\
\midrule
MMLU & Prompt inj.    & $+^{*}$ & $+^{*}$ & Co \\
MMLU & Sycophancy     & $+^{*}$ & $+^{*}$ & Co \\
MMLU & Score manip.   & n.s.    & n.s.    & -- \\
MMLU & Verbosity      & $+^{*}$ & $+^{*}$ & Co \\
MMLU & Position bias  & $+^{*}$ & n.s.    & $\eta$ \\
MMLU & Authority bias & $+^{*}$ & n.s.    & $\eta$ \\
\addlinespace
ARC  & Prompt inj.    & $+^{*}$ & $+^{*}$ & Co \\
ARC  & Sycophancy     & $+^{*}$ & $-^{*}$ & Co \\
ARC  & Score manip.   & n.s.    & $+^{*}$ & $K$ \\
ARC  & Verbosity      & $+^{*}$ & n.s.    & $\eta$ \\
ARC  & Position bias  & n.s.    & $+^{*}$ & $K$ \\
ARC  & Authority bias & $+^{*}$ & $+^{*}$ & Co \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\caption{Standardized regression coefficients for $\eta_{\max}$ and $K_{\mathrm{eff}}$ across 12 conditions (455 panels, log-log OLS). $+^{*}$/$-^{*}$: FDR-significant positive/negative (Benjamini--Hochberg, $q < 0.05$); n.s.: not significant. Dom.\ column: $\eta$ = $\eta_{\max}$ sole dominant, $K$ = $K_{\mathrm{eff}}$ sole dominant, Co = co-dominant \citep{budescu1993dominance}.}
\label{tab:dominance}
\end{table}

$\eta_{\max}$ reaches FDR significance in 9/12 conditions (exceptions: MMLU $\times$ score manipulation, ARC $\times$ score manipulation, and ARC $\times$ position bias), consistent with Theorem~\ref{thm:monotonicity}. $K_{\mathrm{eff}}$ is significant positive in 7/12 and negative in 1 (ARC $\times$ sycophancy). Dominance analysis yields 3 $\eta_{\max}$-dominant, 6 co-dominant, 2 $K_{\mathrm{eff}}$-dominant, and 1 condition where neither predictor reaches significance. However, the $r = 0.70$ $K_{\mathrm{eff}}$--$\eta_{\max}$ correlation raises confound concerns, and OLS ignores the non-independence of the 455 panels.

Each judge appears in $\binom{14}{2} = 91$ of the 455 panels, and each pair co-occurs in 13. This shared structure causes OLS to underestimate standard errors by approximately $2.5{\times}$. Table~\ref{tab:robustness} compares OLS against three corrections.

\begin{table}[t]
\centering
\small
\begin{tabular}{lcccc}
\toprule
\textbf{Method} & $\boldsymbol{\eta_{\max}}$ \textbf{sig} & $\boldsymbol{K_{\mathrm{eff}}}$ \textbf{sig} & $\boldsymbol{K_{\mathrm{eff}}}$ \textbf{dir.} & $\boldsymbol{\eta}$\textbf{:}$\boldsymbol{K}$ \\
\midrule
OLS                   & 9/12  & 8/12 & 7+/1$-$ & 1.1:1 \\
Clustered SE (CR1)    & 8/12  & 0/12 & ---     & ---   \\
Mixed effects         & 7/12  & 3/12 & 3+/0$-$ & 2.3:1 \\
Wild cluster bootstrap & 5/12  & 1/12 & 1+/0$-$ & 5:1   \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\caption{Robustness of regression results to non-independence correction ($K{=}3$). Clustered SE uses 3-position max-SE with CR1 correction (13 clusters per position). Mixed effects uses 3-position max-$p$ with random judge intercepts. Wild cluster bootstrap uses $B{=}9999$ resamples, Rademacher weights, and max-$p$ across 3 panel positions. $\eta$:$K$ ratio = $\eta_{\max}$ significant conditions / $K_{\mathrm{eff}}$ significant conditions.}
\label{tab:robustness}
\end{table}

$\eta_{\max}$ remains robust: 8/12 under clustered SE, 7/12 under mixed effects, 5/12 under wild cluster bootstrap (Figure~\ref{fig:robustness}). $K_{\mathrm{eff}}$ collapses under correction: OLS's 8/12 reduces to 0/12 (clustered SE) and 1/12 (bootstrap). Point estimates remain positive, but confidence intervals contain zero in 11/12 conditions (Appendix~\ref{app:forest_plot}).

\begin{figure}[t]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=\columnwidth]{figures/fig1_robustness_comparison.pdf}
\caption{Significance counts (FDR $< 0.05$) for $\eta_{\max}$ and $K_{\mathrm{eff}}$ across four correction methods over 12 experimental conditions ($K{=}3$). Wild cluster bootstrap and clustered standard errors are recommended for the 15-judge combinatorial design.}
\label{fig:robustness}
\end{figure}

Power analysis yields MDE $\beta_{\mathrm{std}} = 0.14$ at 80\% power (MDE decreases to 0.048 at $K{=}5$ and 0.036 at $K{=}7$; Appendix~\ref{app:power_analysis}). $K_{\mathrm{eff}}$'s non-significance at $K{=}3$ is thus an \emph{informative null}: any true effect is smaller than 0.14 SD.

\subsection{Panel-Size-Dependent Regime Transition}
\label{sec:cross_k}

The $K{=}3$ results above show that $K_{\mathrm{eff}}$ fails to reach significance under corrected inference. A natural question is whether this negligibility is intrinsic to judge diversity or an artifact of small panel size. We address this by repeating the wild cluster bootstrap analysis at $K \in \{3, 5, 7\}$, constructing all $\binom{15}{K}$ panels for each value and applying the same specification (Equation~\ref{eq:regression}) with $K$-position max-$p$ correction.

\begin{table}[t]
\centering
\small
\begin{tabular}{rrccc}
\toprule
$\boldsymbol{K}$ & \textbf{Panels} & $\boldsymbol{\eta_{\max}}$ \textbf{sig} & $\boldsymbol{K_{\mathrm{eff}}}$ \textbf{sig} & $\boldsymbol{K_{\mathrm{eff}}}$ \textbf{dir.} \\
\midrule
3 & 455     & 5/12 & 1/12 & 1+/0$-$ \\
5 & 3{,}003 & 0/12 & 7/12 & 7+/0$-$ \\
7 & 6{,}435 & 0/12 & 5/12 & 5+/0$-$ \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\caption{Cross-$K$ wild cluster bootstrap summary ($B{=}9999$, Rademacher weights, $K$-position max-$p$, BH FDR $\alpha{=}0.05$, $\epsilon{=}10^{-6}$). As panel size increases, $\eta_{\max}$ significance drops from 5/12 to 0/12 while $K_{\mathrm{eff}}$ rises from 1/12 to 7/12. All significant $K_{\mathrm{eff}}$ coefficients are positive.}
\label{tab:cross_k}
\end{table}

Table~\ref{tab:cross_k} reveals a sharp regime transition between $K{=}3$ and $K{=}5$. At $K{=}3$, $\eta_{\max}$ is the dominant predictor (5/12) and $K_{\mathrm{eff}}$ is negligible (1/12). At $K{=}5$, $\eta_{\max}$ drops to 0/12 while $K_{\mathrm{eff}}$ rises to 7/12, all positive. Restricting to the four $K_{\mathrm{eff}}$-stable attacks ($\rho{\geq}0.91$; Appendix~\ref{app:attacked_keff}) yields 4/8 at $K{=}5$, confirming that the signal is not driven by measurement-unstable conditions. The attenuation from 7/12 at $K{=}5$ to 5/12 at $K{=}7$ reflects increased conservatism of the 7-position max-$p$ correction (\S\ref{sec:analysis}).

\subsection{Random vs.\ Adaptive Fault Contrast}
\label{sec:rf_contrast}

We simulate non-adversarial noise by randomly flipping judge votes at observed per-judge error rates. At $K{=}3$, random faults yield $R^2 = 0.73$--$0.81$ with $K_{\mathrm{eff}}$ significant in 2/2 conditions; under adaptive attack, significance collapses (1/12). At $K{=}5$, random faults yield $R^2 = 0.705$ ($K_{\mathrm{eff}}$ 2/2 significant); adaptive attack reduces $R^2$ to 0.256 but $K_{\mathrm{eff}}$ retains predictive power (7/12). The gap narrows from ${\sim}0.6$ ($K{=}3$) to $0.45$ ($K{=}5$), confirming the contrast is most pronounced in small panels.

\begin{figure}[t]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=\columnwidth]{figures/fig2_gam_keff_split.pdf}
\caption{$K_{\mathrm{eff}}$ effect direction split by attack type at $K{=}3$ (GAM with FDR correction). Targeted attacks show positive $K_{\mathrm{eff}}$ (higher diversity associated with higher vulnerability), while shared-bias attacks show negative $K_{\mathrm{eff}}$ (higher diversity associated with lower vulnerability). At $K{=}5$, 11/12 conditions retain this pattern.}
\label{fig:gam_split}
\end{figure}

\subsection{Attack-Type Empirical Categorization}
\label{sec:gam_taxonomy}

GAM analysis reveals structure masked by the significance collapse (Figure~\ref{fig:gam_split}). At $K{=}3$, the 12 conditions split 6:6 by attack mechanism. \textit{Targeted attacks} (prompt injection, score manipulation, position bias) show positive $K_{\mathrm{eff}}$ in all 6 conditions; \textit{shared-bias attacks} (sycophancy, verbosity bias, authority bias) show negative $K_{\mathrm{eff}}$ in all 6. Position bias is an anomalous case within the targeted category: while it shows positive $K_{\mathrm{eff}}$ direction, its $K_{\mathrm{eff}}$ stability is low ($\rho{=}0.08$ on ARC; Appendix~\ref{app:attacked_keff}), placing it closer to a measurement-unstable regime than to the other targeted attacks ($\rho{\geq}0.60$). At $K{=}5$, 11/12 conditions maintain the targeted/shared-bias distinction.

\subsection{Aggregation Rules Modulate the Diversity Channel}
\label{sec:aggregation}

Table~\ref{tab:aggregation} reports regression results for four aggregation methods across all 12 conditions.

\begin{table}[t]
\centering
\small
\begin{tabular}{lcccc}
\toprule
\textbf{Method} & \textbf{$\eta_{\max}$ sig} & \textbf{$K_{\mathrm{eff}}$ sig} & \textbf{$K_{\mathrm{eff}}$ $\beta$ dir.} & \textbf{$R^2$} \\
\midrule
Threshold       & 12/12 & 11/12 & 10+/1$-$  & 0.89 \\
Majority        & 9/12  & 8/12  & 7+/1$-$   & 0.298 \\
Weighted        & 9/12  & 9/12  & 9+/0$-$   & 0.284 \\
Bayesian        & 9/12  & 7/12  & 1+/6$-$   & 0.104 \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\caption{Aggregation method comparison ($K{=}3$, 15 models, 455 panels, log-log canonical spec). Significance counts are FDR-corrected across 12 conditions. $R^2$ is the mean across conditions. Methods are ordered by $R^2$ to highlight the vulnerability gradient from threshold (most predictable) to Bayesian (least).}
\label{tab:aggregation}
\end{table}


The methods form a vulnerability gradient from threshold ($R^2{=}0.89$) to Bayesian ($R^2{=}0.104$; Appendix~\ref{app:aggregation_details}). Threshold voting's high $R^2$ is partly structural: unanimity makes panel failure nearly deterministic given the weakest judge. Bayesian calibration is associated with a sign change in the diversity channel: $K_{\mathrm{eff}}$ direction shifts from predominantly positive (7+/1$-$) to predominantly negative (1+/6$-$).

\appendix

\section{Proof of Theorem~\ref{thm:monotonicity}}
\label{app:proof}

The replaced judge $J_K'$ is \emph{pivotal} exactly when $t{-}1$ of the remaining $K{-}1$ judges have already failed. Formally, let $S = \sum_{j=1}^{K-1} X_j^a$ denote the number of failures among the shared judges. The panel fails if and only if the $K$-th judge also fails when $S = t{-}1$, or when $S \geq t$ regardless of the $K$-th judge:
\begin{align}
\mathrm{ASR}(\mathcal{P}, a) &= \Pr(S \geq t) + \Pr(S = t{-}1) \cdot \Pr(X_K^a {=} 1 \mid S = t{-}1).
\end{align}
Since $\Pr(X_{K'}^a {=} 1 \mid \mathbf{X}_{-K}^a {=} \mathbf{x}_{-K}) \geq \Pr(X_K^a {=} 1 \mid \mathbf{X}_{-K}^a {=} \mathbf{x}_{-K})$ for all attacks $a$ and realizations $\mathbf{x}_{-K}$ (by hypothesis), the second term is weakly larger for $\mathcal{P}'$ than for $\mathcal{P}$ under every attack. Taking the supremum over attacks preserves the inequality: $\mathrm{ASR}^*(\mathcal{P}') \geq \mathrm{ASR}^*(\mathcal{P})$. The result holds under arbitrary inter-judge correlations and any attack space $\mathcal{A}$.

\section{Robustness Correction Methods}
\label{app:robustness_methods}

The 455 panels are constructed combinatorially from 15 shared judges: each judge appears in $\binom{14}{2} = 91$ panels, inducing correlated residuals across panel-level observations. Standard OLS assumes independent errors, potentially inflating significance. We apply three corrections.

\paragraph{Clustered Standard Errors.} Because each panel of $K{=}3$ judges does not map to a single cluster, we perform three separate one-way clusterings (one for each judge position in the ordered panel tuple) using CR1 bias-corrected sandwich estimators (13 clusters per position). The reported standard error for each coefficient is the maximum across the three clusterings, yielding the most conservative inference.

\paragraph{Mixed Effects.} For each of the three judge positions, we fit a linear mixed-effects model with a random intercept for the judge occupying that position, estimating $K_{\mathrm{eff}}$ and $\eta_{\max}$ as fixed effects. The reported $p$-value for each coefficient is the maximum across the three models.

\paragraph{Wild Cluster Bootstrap.} We perform $B{=}9{,}999$ bootstrap resamples using Rademacher weights, clustering by each of the three panel positions in turn and taking the maximum $p$-value across positions (the most conservative aggregation). This approach avoids distributional assumptions on the cluster structure.

\section{Extended Analysis}
\label{app:extended_analysis}

\subsection{BFT Reversal Mechanism: Detailed Evidence}
\label{app:bft_reversal}

Under standard OLS, the regression results (Table~\ref{tab:dominance}) show $\eta_{\max}$ significant in 9 of 12 conditions and $K_{\mathrm{eff}}$ positively associated with ASR in 7. Non-independence correction reveals an asymmetry: $\eta_{\max}$ is significant in 5 to 8 of 12 conditions depending on correction method (clustered SE: 8/12; mixed effects: 7/12; wild cluster bootstrap: 5/12), while $K_{\mathrm{eff}}$ collapses to 0--1/12 (clustered SE 0/12; bootstrap 1/12; mixed effects 3/12). The directional pattern (point estimates predominantly positive) persists, but statistical confidence is limited. The random-fault contrast (\S\ref{sec:rf_contrast}) sharpens this: under random faults, $K_{\mathrm{eff}}$'s positive association with panel error survives all corrections (2/2 conditions, $R^2 = 0.73$--$0.81$), indicating a genuine relationship that becomes artifactual under adaptive attack (0--1/12 after correction).

Under OLS, the sole negative $K_{\mathrm{eff}}$ coefficient is ARC $\times$ sycophancy ($\hat{\beta}_{\mathrm{std}} = -0.192$, FDR $p = 0.013$), though this effect does not survive non-independence correction (clustered $p = 0.441$). The GAM taxonomy subsumes this individual exception: the directional pattern across all 6 shared-bias conditions is the stronger evidence, not any single condition's significance.

\subsection{Diversity--Capability Tradeoff}
\label{app:diversity_capability}

The $r = 0.70$ correlation between $K_{\mathrm{eff}}$ and $\eta_{\max}$ reflects a structural feature of the current LLM ecosystem: maximizing panel diversity requires sampling across capability tiers, which systematically introduces less capable and therefore more vulnerable judges. The structural correlation is unaffected by SE correction; it is a property of the model ecosystem, not an artifact of panel overlap. However, the high confound combined with fragile independent significance means that observational regression cannot reliably disentangle diversity's causal contribution from the capability deficit that co-occurs with it.

Family-level labels provide indirect evidence that capability proximity matters more than taxonomic grouping: family membership is not a significant predictor of panel vulnerability ($p = 0.179$ on MMLU, $p = 0.385$ on ARC), consistent with models from the same provider exhibiting heterogeneous vulnerability profiles that vary by capability tier rather than by shared architecture \citep{kuncheva2003measures}.

\subsection{Aggregation Mechanism Details}
\label{app:aggregation_details}

Bayesian calibration is associated with a shift in the $K_{\mathrm{eff}}$ coefficient from predominantly positive under majority vote to predominantly negative (6 of 7 significant coefficients negative). The mechanism is calibrated reliability weighting: by estimating each judge's confusion matrix on clean data, Bayesian aggregation downweights weak judges, effectively implementing robustness screening within the aggregation rule itself. Threshold voting amplifies both channels because any single dissenting judge vetoes the panel decision, making compositional factors almost perfectly predictive of attack success ($R^2{=}0.89$). Bayesian calibration requires a clean accuracy prior, and an adaptive attacker aware of this calibration could target the prior itself \citep{tambe2011security}.

\begin{figure}[h]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=\columnwidth]{figures/fig3_aggregation_gradient.pdf}
\caption{Distribution of $K_{\mathrm{eff}}$ coefficients across aggregation methods. Bayesian aggregation is associated with a sign change in $K_{\mathrm{eff}}$ direction (1 positive, 6 negative), contrasting sharply with majority voting (7 positive, 1 negative).}
\label{fig:aggregation}
\end{figure}

\subsection{Aggregation at \texorpdfstring{$K{=}5$}{K=5}}
\label{app:aggregation_k5}

Table~\ref{tab:aggregation_k5} extends the aggregation analysis to $K{=}5$ (3{,}003 panels) under wild cluster bootstrap with 5-position max-$p$. Bayesian calibration neutralizes both predictors ($\eta_{\max}$ 1/12, $K_{\mathrm{eff}}$ 0/12, $\bar{R}^2{=}0.081$), contrasting with majority vote where $K_{\mathrm{eff}}$ dominates (7/12). Threshold voting retains high predictability ($\bar{R}^2{=}0.506$) with both predictors significant (6/12 and 10/12). The Bayesian neutralization at $K{=}5$ extends the reversal observed at $K{=}3$ (Table~\ref{tab:aggregation}): calibrated confusion matrices effectively remove both individual weakness and structural diversity as predictive channels.

\begin{table}[h]
\centering
\small
\begin{tabular}{lccc}
\toprule
\textbf{Aggregation} & $\boldsymbol{\eta_{\max}}$ \textbf{sig} & $\boldsymbol{K_{\mathrm{eff}}}$ \textbf{sig} & $\boldsymbol{\bar{R}^2}$ \\
\midrule
Majority   & 0/12  & 7/12  & 0.256 \\
Weighted   & 1/12  & 9/12  & 0.249 \\
Threshold  & 6/12  & 10/12 & 0.506 \\
Bayesian   & 1/12  & 0/12  & 0.081 \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\caption{Aggregation method comparison at $K{=}5$ (3{,}003 panels, wild cluster bootstrap with 5-position max-$p$, BH FDR $\alpha{=}0.05$). Bayesian calibration neutralizes both predictors, while majority vote preserves the $K_{\mathrm{eff}}$-dominant regime transition. Compare Table~\ref{tab:aggregation} ($K{=}3$).}
\label{tab:aggregation_k5}
\end{table}

\subsection{Metric Robustness Under Partial Panel Information}
\label{app:oracle}

The main analysis computes $\eta_{\max}$ with full knowledge of panel composition (all three judges known). In deployment, a practitioner selecting or auditing a panel may know only a subset of the constituent judges. We test whether $\eta_{\max}$ retains predictive power under such partial information by recomputing it from two of the three panel members: for each panel, we cycle through all $\binom{3}{1} = 3$ leave-one-out masks, compute $\eta_{\max}$ from the two known judges, and average the regression estimates across masking scenarios.

Under the most conservative inference (partial information + clustered SE), $\eta_{\max}$ remains the sole significant predictor with $\hat{\beta}_{\mathrm{std}} = 0.589$, achieving significance in 12/12 conditions, stronger than the oracle baseline (8/12 under clustered SE). $K_{\mathrm{eff}}$ loses all predictive power (0/12). The averaging over masking scenarios smooths estimation noise inherent in the three-judge oracle computation, strengthening the $\eta_{\max}$ signal. This demonstrates that $\eta_{\max}$ computed from partial panel information is robust to incomplete composition knowledge and retains (indeed improves) its predictive power for panel ASR.

\section{Panel Selection Strategy Comparison}
\label{app:panel_selection}

\begin{figure}[h]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=\columnwidth]{figures/fig4_panel_design.pdf}
\caption{Panel attack success rate under three selection strategies. $\min(\eta_{\max})$ achieves lowest ASR in 12/12 conditions, confirming that robustness-first selection dominates diversity-maximizing strategies.}
\label{fig:panel_design}
\end{figure}

Figure~\ref{fig:panel_design} compares three panel selection strategies: $\min(\eta_{\max})$ (select the panel minimizing weakest-judge vulnerability), random selection, and $\max(K_{\mathrm{eff}})$ (maximize diversity). The $\min(\eta_{\max})$ strategy achieves the lowest ASR in all 12 conditions, consistent with the weakest-link dominance documented in \S\ref{sec:dominance}.

\section{Forest Plot: Coefficient Estimates with Clustered Standard Errors}
\label{app:forest_plot}

\begin{figure}[h]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=\columnwidth]{figures/fig5_forest_plot.pdf}
